## Project 2: Classic Feedback Tool (SharePoint)
**In-page visual feedback and task-creation tool for SharePoint** | SPFx 1.21 | ~6.2k lines of TypeScript

**One-line summary:**
SharePoint Framework solution jisme reviewer page par kisi bhi area par box draw karta hai, screenshot capture aur annotate hota hai, aur task seedha tenant ki existing task list mein ban jaata hai. Koi provisioning ya naya list nahi chahiye.

**Tech stack:**
- **Platform:** SharePoint Framework (SPFx) 1.21.1, Web Part + Application Customizer (ek hi package, shared code)
- **Frontend:** React 17, TypeScript 5.3, Fluent/SP UI, React Portals
- **Data:** PnPjs v4 (`@pnp/sp`), SharePoint REST, SmartMetadata taxonomy
- **Capture:** html2canvas / html2canvas-pro, modern-screenshot, custom canvas annotation editor
- **Build/Deploy:** Gulp, ESLint, `.sppkg` packaging, tenant App Catalog

**Resume bullets:**
- Built a dual-host SPFx solution (Web Part + Application Customizer) that shares all services and components, so one package works on every page of a site without page edits.
- Built a schema-adaptive task service that reads the target list's actual columns, maps values to what exists, retries after dropping rejected fields, and reports what was dropped. This lets it work across many differently structured task lists.
- Drove sites, categories and priorities dynamically from the tenant's `SmartMetadata` list. Nothing is hard-coded and no provisioning is needed.
- Fixed html2canvas failures on modern CSS (oklch, oklab, color-mix from Tailwind v4) with a document-patching pipeline. Batched colour resolution through a single offscreen-canvas readback, which cut capture time from ~500 ms to ~1 ms.
- Built the area-selection UX: drag-to-capture, element fallback, multi-screenshot support (including popups), and a per-screenshot annotation editor.
- Generated resilient CSS selectors (volatile classes filtered, uniqueness verified) to anchor each task to the element and page it was raised against.
- Solved SharePoint stacking-context issues by rendering through a portal on `<body>` at a very high z-index, avoiding MutationObserver re-append costs.
- Uploaded screenshots as attachments and registered them in the target app's image metadata so they appear in the existing task popup.

